---
// The box every index-list mark is drawn inside — /articles, /projects,
// /visualizations.
//
// WHAT IT IS FOR. Those three pages are the same page in three flavours: a mark
// on the left, a headline and a line or two on the right. The marks are not the
// same as each other and never will be — one is a borrowed plugin logo, one is
// a line icon drawn in 2013, one is an Observable Plot chart — so left to
// themselves they arrive at different sizes and different proportions, and the
// column of headlines beside them comes out ragged. This fixes the box and fits
// whatever it is given inside it.
//
// EVERY MARK IS A SHAPE, NOT A PICTURE. All of them are one colour on nothing,
// so none of them carries a colour worth keeping — and painting them in the
// page's own colour is what lets them follow the theme, and light up with the
// row they belong to. Two ways in, depending on what the mark is:
//
//   • An inline <svg>, passed as a child. Both sources draw in `currentColor`
//     (or carry no fill at all), so `fill` below reaches them.
//   • A `mask` URL, for the article thumbs, which are raster. The box is filled
//     with currentColor and the file is used as a MASK over it, so its alpha
//     becomes the drawing. This replaces a `filter: invert(1)` that used to sit
//     on that page: those icons are black line art drawn for the white Jekyll
//     blog they came from, and flipping them got them visible on a dark page but
//     left them stuck at whatever colour the flip produced. As a mask they are
//     simply a shape, and take the colour like everything else.
//
// FITTING. The box is one size; the mark is contained inside it and centred.
// An inline <svg> at `width: 100%; height: 100%` does this itself — SVG's
// DEFAULT preserveAspectRatio ("xMidYMid meet") fits the viewBox inside and
// centres it, which is the semantics of the element rather than a CSS trick.
// THE ONE REQUIREMENT is that the SVG carries a viewBox; both sources do. A
// mask does the same with `center / contain`.
//
// WHAT IT CANNOT DO is make two marks look the same SIZE, only occupy the same
// box — a drawing with empty margin baked into its own frame contains to its
// margin and reads small. That is why src/assets/thumbs/* are trimmed to their
// ink (they were not, and the marks on /articles ranged from 27% to 65% of
// their box); a new thumb has to be trimmed the same way. See the note on
// `thumb` in src/content.config.ts.
interface Props {
  /**
   * The box's width, as any CSS length. The pages set their own — /articles and
   * /projects share one so the two lists line up with each other; the chart
   * index runs larger because its marks carry more detail.
   */
  size: string;
  /**
   * The box's aspect ratio, width ÷ height. Square by default, which is what a
   * list of unlike marks wants.
   *
   * /visualizations passes 8/5 instead, and that is not an inconsistency: every
   * mark on that page is emitted by the same loader at the same 128 × 80, so
   * they are already identical to each other, and a square box would only add a
   * band of empty space above and below each one.
   */
  ratio?: number;
  /**
   * URL of a file to paint as a shape. For the article thumbs, which are raster
   * and so cannot be handed a colour any other way — see above. Leave it unset
   * and pass an inline <svg> as a child instead.
   */
  mask?: string;
  /**
   * The masked file's own aspect ratio, width ÷ height.
   *
   * Required alongside `mask`, and it is what makes the mark a HOVER TARGET the
   * size of the drawing rather than the size of the box. A mask is a painting
   * operation: it does not affect hit testing, so a masked square box is
   * hoverable in all four empty corners. Sizing the masked element to the
   * drawing instead puts the pointer target where the ink is. (An inline <svg>
   * needs none of this — see the pointer-events note in the stylesheet.)
   */
  inkRatio?: number;
}

const { size, ratio = 1, mask, inkRatio = 1 } = Astro.props;
const geometry = `--mark-size:${size};--mark-ratio:${ratio}`;

// Which axis binds when the drawing is contained inside the box. Worked out
// here rather than left to `max-inline-size`/`max-block-size`, because an
// element sized by `aspect-ratio` needs exactly ONE definite dimension — give it
// two bounds and the ratio has nothing left to derive.
const inkBox = inkRatio >= ratio ? "--ink-w:100%;--ink-h:auto" : "--ink-w:auto;--ink-h:100%";
---

{
  mask ? (
    <div class="index-mark index-mark--mask" style={geometry}>
      <span
        class="index-mark__ink"
        style={`--mark-mask:url("${mask}");--ink-ratio:${inkRatio};${inkBox}`}
      />
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="index-mark" style={geometry}>
      <slot />
    </div>
  )
}

<style>
  .index-mark {
    /* Never shrink: these sit in a flex row beside text that will happily take
       every pixel it is offered. */
    flex: none;
    inline-size: var(--mark-size);
    aspect-ratio: var(--mark-ratio);
    /* Nudged down so the mark's optical centre sits with the headline's x-height
       rather than its cap line. */
    margin-block-start: 0.3rem;

    /* COLOUR AND WEIGHT COME FROM ABOVE, and that is the whole mechanism behind
       the hover. Custom properties inherit, so the row sets these on ITSELF —
       `.article`, `.project`, `.viz-index__item` — and never has to select this
       element. Which matters more than it sounds: a page CANNOT style this box
       through a class. Astro scopes `.article__mark` to that page's id, this
       box only carries THIS file's id, and the rule silently never matches.
       That is exactly how the article marks went invisible once (a page-scoped
       `filter: invert(1)` that never applied, on black art over a black page). */
    color: var(--mark-color, var(--text));
    fill: currentColor;
    opacity: var(--mark-opacity, 0.85);
    /* The same duration and easing as .nav-btn in src/styles/chrome.css, so a
       mark lighting up and the header's button filling in read as one idiom. */
    transition:
      color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out),
      opacity var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  .index-mark--mask {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
  }

  /* The drawing: a flat fill of the current colour, cut to the file's alpha.
     Sized to the ink rather than to the box (see `inkRatio`), so `contain` here
     is belt and braces against rounding rather than the thing doing the fitting.
     -webkit- first, for Safari. */
  .index-mark__ink {
    inline-size: var(--ink-w);
    block-size: var(--ink-h);
    aspect-ratio: var(--ink-ratio);
    background: currentColor;
    -webkit-mask: var(--mark-mask) center / contain no-repeat;
    mask: var(--mark-mask) center / contain no-repeat;
  }

  /* :global because the SVGs arrive two ways Astro does not scope to this file —
     inlined from set:html, and from an imported .svg component. */
  .index-mark > :global(svg) {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  /* ── ONLY THE DRAWING IS A HOVER TARGET ──────────────────────────────────
     Not the empty corner of the box around it. The entry that owns this mark
     lights as a whole — the drawing, the headline and the description together,
     from a hover on any one of them (see `:has(:hover)` on each index page) —
     and this is what keeps "any one of them" from including thirty pixels of
     empty margin. The box is taken out of hit testing entirely and only what is
     drawn inside it put back:

       • An inline <svg> hands `visiblePainted` to its SHAPES — SVG's own rule
         that a pointer counts as over an element only where it is actually
         painted, so the letterboxing either side of a chart mark and the gaps
         inside a line drawing are dead space. Per-pixel, for free, from the
         format.

         On the SHAPES and not on the <svg> itself, which is the whole trick:
         an inline <svg> in HTML is a replaced element and hit-tests as its
         BORDER BOX no matter what `pointer-events` says, so `visiblePainted`
         there is silently ignored and every empty corner stays a target
         (verified — it was). The root is taken out instead, and the children
         put back; `pointer-events` inherits, so one rule on the direct children
         reaches every shape however deeply it is nested.
       • The masked span is a rectangle to hit testing no matter what the mask
         does, so the best available is a rectangle the size of the drawing —
         which is what `inkRatio` arranges. The same limit reaches three of the
         chart marks: glucose, hospital-prices and new-york-real-estate are a
         PNG carried as a mask over one full-size <rect> (see maskedRaster in
         viz/src/thumbs/mark.js), so that rect is painted everywhere and their
         whole box counts. The other five marks are vector and are exact.

     `pointer-events: none` on the box does not make the entry unclickable: the
     hit falls through to the link wrapping the whole row, so a click on an empty
     corner still follows it. */
  .index-mark {
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .index-mark > :global(svg) {
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .index-mark > :global(svg) > :global(*) {
    pointer-events: visiblePainted;
  }

  .index-mark__ink {
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .index-mark {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
